  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(8,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\u27e88,3,-1\u27e9.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\u27e84,3,-1\u27e9.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=\u27e84,3,-1\u27e9.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e84,3,-1\u27e9 & =\u27e88,3,-1\u27e9\\cdot \u27e84,3,-1\u27e9 \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e84,3,-1\u27e9 & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9\\cdot \u27e84,3,-1\u27e9 & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z-42 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
